WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.730
Sometimes the app is the whole story.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.150
But as a solution grows, it picks up more moving parts:

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.070
Dataverse tables, Copilot Studio agents, and workflows.

00:00:10.979 --> 00:00:12.609
Those don't live in the app's code.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:16.012
They live in the environment, packaged in a solution,

00:00:16.022 --> 00:00:18.889
and they need to move from dev to test to prod too.

00:00:19.736 --> 00:00:21.986
So the solution rides along with the code.

00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:24.468
It's exported as managed from dev,

00:00:24.478 --> 00:00:27.946
committed to the same branch, and promoted by the same merge.

00:00:28.877 --> 00:00:31.187
Before each deploy, the target is checked:

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:34.927
the solution installed there must match the version in the branch.

00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:39.867
If it doesn't, the deploy stops, so code and schema never drift apart.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.660
If that sounds familiar, it should.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.825
A classic Power App with Azure functions and APIs behin

00:00:46.835 --> 00:00:50.360
d it already moves several pieces through ALM, in step.

00:00:51.213 --> 00:00:56.523
So start simple, and add solutions when Dataverse, agents or workflows join in.

00:00:57.053 --> 00:01:00.223
The complexity follows your architecture, not the platform.

00:01:00.773 --> 00:01:03.683
It's the same discipline enterprise teams use today.
